{% extends "doc_page.jinja" %}
{% import "chrome.jinja" as chrome %}
{% import "components.jinja" as ui %}
{% import "forthcoming_data.jinja" as fc %}
{% block page_title %}Netsukefile Linter (Forthcoming) - Netsuke Build System{% endblock %}
{% block content %}

    <div class="site-container pt-16 grow flex">

        {{ fc.sidebar('linter') }}

        <!-- Main Content -->
        <main id="main-content" class="hm-docs-content flex-1 min-w-0 py-12 px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-12">

            {% call fc.header('linter') %}
                    A manifest that parses, lowers, and produces a valid <code>build.ninja</code> can still be a bad manifest: it rebuilds
                    everything every time, breaks where <code>/bin/sh</code> is not <code>bash</code>, or races on an undeclared edge.
                    <code>netsuke check</code> polices the band between &ldquo;parses&rdquo; and &ldquo;provably wrong&rdquo; with a semantic
                    analysis over Netsuke's own compiler stages, inspired by mbake.
            {% endcall %}

            <section id="status" class="mb-16">
                <div class="bg-warning/5 border border-warning/30 rounded-xl p-6">
                    <h2 class="font-bold text-base-content text-sm uppercase tracking-wide mb-3 flex items-center">
                        <span class="iconify text-warning mr-2" data-icon="carbon:information-filled" aria-hidden="true"></span> Status on {{ generated_at.day }} {{ generated_at.strftime('%B %Y') }}
                    </h2>
                    <ul class="space-y-2 text-sm text-charcoal-mid">
                        <li class="flex items-start"><span class="iconify text-accent mt-1 mr-2 text-xs" data-icon="carbon:close" aria-hidden="true"></span> <span><strong>Not in v{{ netsuke_version }}.</strong> The released CLI has no <code>check</code> command; the roadmap lists it under the <a href="https://github.com/leynos/netsuke/blob/main/docs/roadmap.md#315-canonical-cli-redesign" class="hm-link">canonical CLI redesign</a> (task 3.15.1).</span></li>
                        <li class="flex items-start"><span class="iconify text-success mt-1 mr-2 text-xs" data-icon="carbon:checkmark" aria-hidden="true"></span> <span><strong>Designed.</strong> <a href="https://github.com/leynos/netsuke/blob/issue-592-v0-4-0-design-and-implement-a-netsukefile-linter-inspired-by-mbake/docs/adr-021-manifest-linting-under-netsuke-check.md" class="hm-link">ADR-021</a> (accepted 30 August 2026) places linting under <code>netsuke check</code>. The <a href="https://github.com/leynos/netsuke/blob/issue-592-v0-4-0-design-and-implement-a-netsukefile-linter-inspired-by-mbake/docs/netsuke-linter-rules.md" class="hm-link">rule reference</a> is the source of truth for every rule; the <a href="https://github.com/leynos/netsuke/blob/issue-592-v0-4-0-design-and-implement-a-netsukefile-linter-inspired-by-mbake/docs/netsuke-linter-design.md" class="hm-link">design document</a> is a living document that changes as the prototype is used. Both live on the branch of pull request <a href="https://github.com/leynos/netsuke/pull/621" class="hm-link">#621</a> until it merges, so the links point there. The ADR&rsquo;s number may change on merge, because main has since taken ADR-021 for another decision.</span></li>
                        <li class="flex items-start"><span class="iconify text-warning mt-1 mr-2 text-xs" data-icon="carbon:branch" aria-hidden="true"></span> <span><strong>Implemented on a branch.</strong> Pull request <a href="https://github.com/leynos/netsuke/pull/621" class="hm-link">#621</a>, for issue <a href="https://github.com/leynos/netsuke/issues/592" class="hm-link">#592</a>, carries the command, all twenty-four rules, the suppression scanner, and contract tests that check the rule reference against the registry in both directions. It currently conflicts with main and needs a rebase before it can merge.</span></li>
                        <li class="flex items-start"><span class="iconify text-primary mt-1 mr-2 text-xs" data-icon="carbon:tag" aria-hidden="true"></span> <span><strong>Preview targeted at v0.4.0, as a prototype.</strong> Rule names are permanent from v0.4.0. Everything else may still change: rule membership, default severities, flags, and output shapes. A later step of the branch&rsquo;s <a href="https://github.com/leynos/netsuke/blob/issue-592-v0-4-0-design-and-implement-a-netsukefile-linter-inspired-by-mbake/docs/roadmap.md#12-manifest-linting" class="hm-link">manifest-linting phase</a> freezes those contracts once the rules have been used on manifests their authors did not write.</span></li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
            </section>

            <section id="why" class="mb-16">
                <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl font-bold text-base-content mb-6 flex items-center">
                    <span class="iconify text-primary mr-3 text-lg" data-icon="carbon:idea" aria-hidden="true"></span> Why a linter, and why under <code class="ml-2">check</code>
                </h2>
                <p class="text-charcoal-mid mb-6 max-w-3xl">
                    Netsuke already rejects provable errors: missing rules, duplicate outputs, cycles, and Ninja text it cannot emit.
                    The linter covers what compiles but misbehaves. It is a set of named rules over the compiler's own artefacts &mdash;
                    the authored source with its spans, the expanded manifest, and the lowered build graph &mdash; not a text pass over YAML,
                    so a rule can tell an order-only directory dependency from a content dependency, or recognize that a literal path
                    in a recipe is another target's output.
                </p>
                <div class="grid md:grid-cols-3 gap-6">
                    <div class="bg-white p-6 rounded-xl border border-base-300 shadow-xs min-w-0">
                        <h3 class="font-bold text-base-content mb-2 text-sm">Prior art</h3>
                        <p class="text-xs text-charcoal-mid">mbake confirmed the formatting-versus-semantic split, and its per-rule Boolean configuration and GNU error format shaped the policy table and compact human output. hadolint contributed stable identifiers, per-rule severity, and the failure threshold; buildifier the statement-scoped disable comment; ShellCheck the per-rule URL; Clippy and Ruff the idea that category is metadata and machine output is first-class.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="bg-white p-6 rounded-xl border border-base-300 shadow-xs min-w-0">
                        <h3 class="font-bold text-base-content mb-2 text-sm">No new noun</h3>
                        <p class="text-xs text-charcoal-mid"><code>check</code> was already reserved in the canonical command vocabulary and listed as unbuilt work. A separate <code>lint</code> command would be a synonym the CLI design forbids, and <code>generate --lint</code> would bury findings behind a generation flag. The linter <em>is</em> <code>netsuke check</code>.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="bg-white p-6 rounded-xl border border-base-300 shadow-xs min-w-0">
                        <h3 class="font-bold text-base-content mb-2 text-sm">Constraints</h3>
                        <p class="text-xs text-charcoal-mid">Non-interactive and deterministic: no prompts, network, clock, or terminal dependence, so two runs over the same manifest and policy produce byte-identical findings in a fixed order. No reparsing. No duplication of a hard error. Bounded output. And findings never mutate anything: there is no <code>--fix</code>; automated rewriting of an executable manifest is a separate project.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </section>

            <section id="command" class="mb-16 pt-10 border-t border-stone-light">
                <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl font-bold text-base-content mb-6 flex items-center">
                    <span class="iconify text-primary mr-3 text-lg" data-icon="carbon:terminal" aria-hidden="true"></span> The command
                </h2>
                <p class="text-charcoal-mid mb-6 max-w-3xl">
                    <code>netsuke check</code> analyses the manifest selected by the existing root <code>--file</code> and <code>--directory</code>
                    flags, and adds four flags of its own. Machine output uses the global <code>--json</code>, not a check-specific flag.
                </p>
                <div class="bg-base-content rounded-xl overflow-hidden mb-8">
                    <div class="bg-charcoal-mid/20 px-4 py-2 border-b border-white/10 text-xs font-mono text-stone-light">Invocations from the design document</div>
                    {% highlight 'netsuke-console' %}
$ netsuke check
$ netsuke check --explain
$ netsuke check --explain directory-dep-not-order-only
$ netsuke check --rule clarity=off --rule literal-recipe-path=error --fail-on warning
$ netsuke --json check
{% endhighlight %}
                </div>
                <div class="bg-white rounded-lg border border-base-300 shadow-xs overflow-x-auto [contain:inline-size] mb-6" tabindex="0">
                    <table class="min-w-full hm-rows prop-table">
                        <thead class="bg-base-100">
                            <tr>
                                <th scope="col" class="px-6 py-3 text-left text-xs font-bold text-base-content uppercase tracking-wider">Flag</th>
                                <th scope="col" class="px-6 py-3 text-left text-xs font-bold text-base-content uppercase tracking-wider">Purpose</th>
                                <th scope="col" class="px-6 py-3 text-left text-xs font-bold text-base-content uppercase tracking-wider">Default</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody class="bg-white hm-rows">
                            <tr>
                                <td class="px-6 py-4 whitespace-nowrap text-sm font-mono font-medium text-primary">--rule &lt;NAME=SEVERITY&gt;</td>
                                <td class="px-6 py-4 text-sm text-charcoal-mid">Repeatable policy selector. <code>NAME</code> is a rule or a category; <code>SEVERITY</code> is <code>off</code>, <code>advice</code>, <code>warning</code>, or <code>error</code>. Selectors apply in order, so a category selector followed by a rule selector narrows it. An unknown name is an error, not a warning.</td>
                                <td class="px-6 py-4 whitespace-nowrap text-xs font-mono text-charcoal-mid">none</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td class="px-6 py-4 whitespace-nowrap text-sm font-mono font-medium text-primary">--fail-on &lt;SEVERITY&gt;</td>
                                <td class="px-6 py-4 text-sm text-charcoal-mid">Threshold at which findings fail the command: <code>error</code>, <code>warning</code>, <code>advice</code>, or <code>never</code>.</td>
                                <td class="px-6 py-4 whitespace-nowrap text-xs font-mono text-charcoal-mid">error</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td class="px-6 py-4 whitespace-nowrap text-sm font-mono font-medium text-primary">--limit &lt;N&gt;</td>
                                <td class="px-6 py-4 text-sm text-charcoal-mid">Maximum findings reported, keeping the first in source order; <code>0</code> reports everything. The verdict is decided before bounding, so truncation never changes the exit code.</td>
                                <td class="px-6 py-4 whitespace-nowrap text-xs font-mono text-charcoal-mid">200</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td class="px-6 py-4 whitespace-nowrap text-sm font-mono font-medium text-primary">--explain [NAME]</td>
                                <td class="px-6 py-4 text-sm text-charcoal-mid">Print the rule reference for one rule, or the whole catalogue, instead of analysing. With <code>--json</code> the registry is emitted as <code>result.rules</code> so an editor or agent can build a rule picker without scraping prose.</td>
                                <td class="px-6 py-4 whitespace-nowrap text-xs font-mono text-charcoal-mid">&mdash;</td>
                            </tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
                <div class="bg-primary/5 border border-primary/20 rounded-lg p-5 flex items-start">
                    <span class="iconify text-primary mt-1 mr-3" data-icon="carbon:information" aria-hidden="true"></span>
                    <p class="text-xs text-charcoal-mid">
                        <strong>Exit codes.</strong> <code>0</code> when no finding reaches the threshold, <code>1</code> when one does. A failure to analyse &mdash; missing manifest, parse error, unknown rule name &mdash; is an ordinary command error and also exits <code>1</code> with a diagnostic document. Separating those classes waits on the roadmap's exit-code taxonomy.
                    </p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <section id="rules" class="mb-16 pt-10 border-t border-stone-light">
                <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl font-bold text-base-content mb-6 flex items-center">
                    <span class="iconify text-primary mr-3 text-lg" data-icon="carbon:rule-test" aria-hidden="true"></span> Rule catalogue
                </h2>
                <p class="text-charcoal-mid mb-6 max-w-3xl">
                    Twenty-four rules across nine categories. A rule is a stable, self-describing kebab-case name that is unique across every
                    stage and category; the name is what appears in policy selectors, suppression directives, <code>--explain</code>, and the
                    <code>code</code> and span <code>label</code> of each JSON finding, and it never changes. Category is metadata, so recategorizing a rule cannot
                    invalidate a configuration file. Each finding also carries a diagnostic code of the form
                    <code>netsuke::lint::&lt;name_in_snake_case&gt;</code> and a <code>url</code> into the rule reference.
                </p>
                <div class="bg-white rounded-lg border border-base-300 shadow-xs overflow-x-auto [contain:inline-size] mb-8" tabindex="0">
                    <table class="min-w-full hm-rows prop-table text-sm">
                        <thead class="bg-base-100">
                            <tr>
                                <th scope="col" class="px-4 py-3 text-left text-xs font-bold text-base-content uppercase tracking-wider">Rule</th>
                                <th scope="col" class="px-4 py-3 text-left text-xs font-bold text-base-content uppercase tracking-wider">Category</th>
                                <th scope="col" class="px-4 py-3 text-left text-xs font-bold text-base-content uppercase tracking-wider">Stage</th>
                                <th scope="col" class="px-4 py-3 text-left text-xs font-bold text-base-content uppercase tracking-wider">Default</th>
                                <th scope="col" class="px-4 py-3 text-left text-xs font-bold text-base-content uppercase tracking-wider">Detects</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody class="bg-white hm-rows text-charcoal-mid">
                            <tr><td class="px-4 py-2 font-mono text-xs text-primary whitespace-nowrap">undeclared-target-input</td><td class="px-4 py-2 text-xs">correctness</td><td class="px-4 py-2 text-xs">graph</td><td class="px-4 py-2 text-xs">warning</td><td class="px-4 py-2 text-xs">recipe names another target's output without depending on it</td></tr>
                            <tr><td class="px-4 py-2 font-mono text-xs text-primary whitespace-nowrap">directory-dep-not-order-only</td><td class="px-4 py-2 text-xs">caching</td><td class="px-4 py-2 text-xs">manifest</td><td class="px-4 py-2 text-xs">warning</td><td class="px-4 py-2 text-xs">directory-creating target used as a content dependency</td></tr>
                            <tr><td class="px-4 py-2 font-mono text-xs text-primary whitespace-nowrap">phony-dep-of-file-target</td><td class="px-4 py-2 text-xs">caching</td><td class="px-4 py-2 text-xs">manifest</td><td class="px-4 py-2 text-xs">warning</td><td class="px-4 py-2 text-xs">file target depends on a phony target through <code>sources</code> or <code>deps</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td class="px-4 py-2 font-mono text-xs text-primary whitespace-nowrap">bashism</td><td class="px-4 py-2 text-xs">portability</td><td class="px-4 py-2 text-xs">document</td><td class="px-4 py-2 text-xs">warning</td><td class="px-4 py-2 text-xs">recipe uses a construct <code>/bin/sh</code> does not promise</td></tr>
                            <tr><td class="px-4 py-2 font-mono text-xs text-primary whitespace-nowrap">background-job</td><td class="px-4 py-2 text-xs">determinism</td><td class="px-4 py-2 text-xs">document</td><td class="px-4 py-2 text-xs">warning</td><td class="px-4 py-2 text-xs">recipe detaches a process with a trailing <code>&amp;</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td class="px-4 py-2 font-mono text-xs text-primary whitespace-nowrap">recursive-build-invocation</td><td class="px-4 py-2 text-xs">determinism</td><td class="px-4 py-2 text-xs">document</td><td class="px-4 py-2 text-xs">warning</td><td class="px-4 py-2 text-xs">recipe invokes a build tool</td></tr>
                            <tr><td class="px-4 py-2 font-mono text-xs text-primary whitespace-nowrap">builtin-clean-action</td><td class="px-4 py-2 text-xs">redundancy</td><td class="px-4 py-2 text-xs">document</td><td class="px-4 py-2 text-xs">advice</td><td class="px-4 py-2 text-xs">action named <code>clean</code> duplicates the built-in <code>netsuke clean</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td class="px-4 py-2 font-mono text-xs text-primary whitespace-nowrap">duplicate-rule-recipe</td><td class="px-4 py-2 text-xs">redundancy</td><td class="px-4 py-2 text-xs">manifest</td><td class="px-4 py-2 text-xs">warning</td><td class="px-4 py-2 text-xs">two rules declare identical recipes</td></tr>
                            <tr><td class="px-4 py-2 font-mono text-xs text-primary whitespace-nowrap">redundant-always</td><td class="px-4 py-2 text-xs">redundancy</td><td class="px-4 py-2 text-xs">document</td><td class="px-4 py-2 text-xs">advice</td><td class="px-4 py-2 text-xs"><code>always</code> declared on a target that is already phony</td></tr>
                            <tr><td class="px-4 py-2 font-mono text-xs text-primary whitespace-nowrap">redundant-dependency</td><td class="px-4 py-2 text-xs">redundancy</td><td class="px-4 py-2 text-xs">manifest</td><td class="px-4 py-2 text-xs">advice</td><td class="px-4 py-2 text-xs">path declared under more than one dependency key</td></tr>
                            <tr><td class="px-4 py-2 font-mono text-xs text-primary whitespace-nowrap">serial-order-without-deps</td><td class="px-4 py-2 text-xs">redundancy</td><td class="px-4 py-2 text-xs">document</td><td class="px-4 py-2 text-xs">advice</td><td class="px-4 py-2 text-xs"><code>dependency_order: serial</code> declared with fewer than two <code>deps</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td class="px-4 py-2 font-mono text-xs text-primary whitespace-nowrap">unused-macro</td><td class="px-4 py-2 text-xs">hygiene</td><td class="px-4 py-2 text-xs">document</td><td class="px-4 py-2 text-xs">warning</td><td class="px-4 py-2 text-xs">declared macro that nothing calls</td></tr>
                            <tr><td class="px-4 py-2 font-mono text-xs text-primary whitespace-nowrap">unused-rule</td><td class="px-4 py-2 text-xs">hygiene</td><td class="px-4 py-2 text-xs">manifest</td><td class="px-4 py-2 text-xs">warning</td><td class="px-4 py-2 text-xs">declared rule that no target or action references</td></tr>
                            <tr><td class="px-4 py-2 font-mono text-xs text-primary whitespace-nowrap">unused-var</td><td class="px-4 py-2 text-xs">hygiene</td><td class="px-4 py-2 text-xs">document</td><td class="px-4 py-2 text-xs">warning</td><td class="px-4 py-2 text-xs">global <code>vars</code> entry that no template references</td></tr>
                            <tr><td class="px-4 py-2 font-mono text-xs text-primary whitespace-nowrap">action-without-description</td><td class="px-4 py-2 text-xs">clarity</td><td class="px-4 py-2 text-xs">document</td><td class="px-4 py-2 text-xs">advice</td><td class="px-4 py-2 text-xs">action declares no <code>description</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td class="px-4 py-2 font-mono text-xs text-primary whitespace-nowrap">command-chain-not-list</td><td class="px-4 py-2 text-xs">clarity</td><td class="px-4 py-2 text-xs">document</td><td class="px-4 py-2 text-xs">advice</td><td class="px-4 py-2 text-xs">scalar <code>command</code> chains steps with <code>&amp;&amp;</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td class="px-4 py-2 font-mono text-xs text-primary whitespace-nowrap">literal-recipe-path</td><td class="px-4 py-2 text-xs">clarity</td><td class="px-4 py-2 text-xs">document</td><td class="px-4 py-2 text-xs">warning</td><td class="px-4 py-2 text-xs">recipe repeats a path the target already declares</td></tr>
                            <tr><td class="px-4 py-2 font-mono text-xs text-primary whitespace-nowrap">rule-without-description</td><td class="px-4 py-2 text-xs">clarity</td><td class="px-4 py-2 text-xs">document</td><td class="px-4 py-2 text-xs">off</td><td class="px-4 py-2 text-xs">rule declares no <code>description</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td class="px-4 py-2 font-mono text-xs text-primary whitespace-nowrap">unreachable-target</td><td class="px-4 py-2 text-xs">clarity</td><td class="px-4 py-2 text-xs">graph</td><td class="px-4 py-2 text-xs">off</td><td class="px-4 py-2 text-xs">target reachable from no default and no other target</td></tr>
                            <tr><td class="px-4 py-2 font-mono text-xs text-primary whitespace-nowrap">legacy-placeholder</td><td class="px-4 py-2 text-xs">migration</td><td class="px-4 py-2 text-xs">document</td><td class="px-4 py-2 text-xs">warning</td><td class="px-4 py-2 text-xs">recipe uses the undocumented <code>$in</code> or <code>$out</code> placeholder (see the note below)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td class="px-4 py-2 font-mono text-xs text-primary whitespace-nowrap">manual-ninja-escape</td><td class="px-4 py-2 text-xs">migration</td><td class="px-4 py-2 text-xs">document</td><td class="px-4 py-2 text-xs">warning</td><td class="px-4 py-2 text-xs">recipe doubles a dollar to escape it for Ninja</td></tr>
                            <tr><td class="px-4 py-2 font-mono text-xs text-primary whitespace-nowrap">suppression-without-reason</td><td class="px-4 py-2 text-xs">suppression</td><td class="px-4 py-2 text-xs">directive</td><td class="px-4 py-2 text-xs">warning</td><td class="px-4 py-2 text-xs">lint directive states no reason</td></tr>
                            <tr><td class="px-4 py-2 font-mono text-xs text-primary whitespace-nowrap">unknown-suppression</td><td class="px-4 py-2 text-xs">suppression</td><td class="px-4 py-2 text-xs">directive</td><td class="px-4 py-2 text-xs">warning</td><td class="px-4 py-2 text-xs">lint directive names a rule that does not exist</td></tr>
                            <tr><td class="px-4 py-2 font-mono text-xs text-primary whitespace-nowrap">unused-suppression</td><td class="px-4 py-2 text-xs">suppression</td><td class="px-4 py-2 text-xs">directive</td><td class="px-4 py-2 text-xs">advice</td><td class="px-4 py-2 text-xs">lint directive suppressed no finding</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
                <p class="text-xs text-charcoal-mid mb-8">Rules default to <code>off</code> only when they encode a project convention rather than a defect: building a target by name without declaring it a default is a legitimate workflow, and rule descriptions are a house style. The two migration rules catch stale workarounds. Doubling a dollar (<code>$$PATH</code>) became a bug once Netsuke escaped dollars at the Ninja writer (ADR-014, shipped in v0.1.0-beta3); see <a href="/netsuke/docs/security/#shell-dollars" class="hm-link">markers and shell dollars</a>. On the branch, <code>legacy-placeholder</code> flags <code>$in</code>/<code>$out</code> because they were still substituted during lowering while the users&rsquo; guide documented only <code>{{ '{{' }} ins {{ '}}' }}</code> and <code>{{ '{{' }} outs {{ '}}' }}</code>. Main has since gone further: <a href="https://github.com/leynos/netsuke/blob/main/docs/adr-034-preserve-script-in-out-as-shell-variables.md" class="hm-link">ADR-034</a> makes <code>$in</code> and <code>$out</code> plain shell variables in every recipe, so this rule&rsquo;s rationale will need revising when #621 rebases.</p>

                <h3 class="font-bold text-base-content mb-4 text-sm uppercase tracking-wide">One rule, worked</h3>
                <p class="text-sm text-charcoal-mid mb-4 max-w-3xl"><code>undeclared-target-input</code>: Ninja schedules edges concurrently unless a dependency orders them. A recipe that reads a path another target produces, without declaring it, races &mdash; it succeeds whenever the producer happened to run first. Serial local builds hide this; a parallel or clean build does not.</p>
                <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-6">
                    <div>
                        <p class="text-xs font-mono uppercase tracking-wide text-accent mb-2">Reported</p>
                        <div class="bg-base-content rounded-lg p-4 overflow-x-auto min-w-0 [contain:inline-size]" tabindex="0">
{% highlight 'netsuke' %}{% raw %}
targets:
  - name: app
    command: "cc build/main.o -o {{ outs }}"
  - name: build/main.o
    command: "cc -c src/main.c -o {{ outs }}"
{% endraw %}{% endhighlight %}
                        </div>
                    </div>
                    <div>
                        <p class="text-xs font-mono uppercase tracking-wide text-success mb-2">Fixed</p>
                        <div class="bg-base-content rounded-lg p-4 overflow-x-auto min-w-0 [contain:inline-size]" tabindex="0">
{% highlight 'netsuke' %}{% raw %}
targets:
  - name: app
    sources: build/main.o
    command: "cc {{ ins }} -o {{ outs }}"
  - name: build/main.o
    command: "cc -c src/main.c -o {{ outs }}"
{% endraw %}{% endhighlight %}
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <p class="text-xs text-charcoal-mid mt-3">Remediation, as the rule reference states it: declare the path under <code>sources</code> if the recipe reads it, or under <code>deps</code> if it only needs it to exist.</p>
            </section>

            <section id="policy" class="mb-16 pt-10 border-t border-stone-light">
                <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl font-bold text-base-content mb-6 flex items-center">
                    <span class="iconify text-primary mr-3 text-lg" data-icon="carbon:settings-adjust" aria-hidden="true"></span> Policy and suppression
                </h2>
                <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-8 mb-8">
                    <div>
                        <h3 class="font-bold text-base-content mb-3 text-sm uppercase tracking-wide">Project policy</h3>
                        <p class="text-sm text-charcoal-mid mb-4">Both policy flags layer through the existing configuration precedence, so a project can fix its policy in <code>netsuke.toml</code>. Setting a severity on an off-by-default rule enables it; setting <code>off</code> on any rule disables it. Nothing about policy resolution consults the environment beyond that chain, reads the terminal, or varies with time.</p>
                        <div class="bg-base-content rounded-lg p-4 overflow-x-auto min-w-0 [contain:inline-size]" tabindex="0">
{% highlight 'toml' %}
[cmds.check]
rule = ["clarity=off", "unreachable-target=warning"]
fail_on = "warning"
{% endhighlight %}
                        </div>
                    </div>
                    <div>
                        <h3 class="font-bold text-base-content mb-3 text-sm uppercase tracking-wide">Suppression directives</h3>
                        <p class="text-sm text-charcoal-mid mb-4">Suppression is narrow by construction: a directive names one or more rules and must state a reason. There is no blanket disable comment and no <code>all</code> selector. Scoping follows YAML indentation: a directive alone on its line governs the next declaration and everything indented beneath it, and a directive at the end of a line governs that line&rsquo;s declaration.</p>
                        <div class="bg-base-content rounded-lg p-4 overflow-x-auto min-w-0 [contain:inline-size]" tabindex="0">
{% highlight 'netsuke' %}
targets:
  # netsuke-lint: allow background-job -- the previewer is intentionally detached
  - name: preview
    script: |
      feh processed &
{% endhighlight %}
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <div class="bg-white p-6 rounded-xl border border-base-300 shadow-xs min-w-0">
                    <h3 class="font-bold text-base-content mb-3 text-sm">Directive grammar</h3>
                    <ul class="space-y-2 text-xs text-charcoal-mid font-mono">
                        <li><code># netsuke-lint: allow &lt;rule&gt;[, &lt;rule&gt;…] -- &lt;reason&gt;</code> <span class="font-sans">suppresses the named rules within one node.</span></li>
                        <li><code># netsuke-lint-file: allow &lt;rule&gt;[, &lt;rule&gt;…] -- &lt;reason&gt;</code> <span class="font-sans">suppresses them for the whole file, for findings that cannot be resolved to a span.</span></li>
                    </ul>
                    <p class="text-xs text-charcoal-mid mt-3 font-sans">A <code>#</code> inside a quoted or block scalar is content, not a directive: the scanner consults the span index, which is why shell comments inside <code>script: |</code> blocks cannot accidentally disable rules. Three rules police the directives themselves &mdash; <code>unknown-suppression</code>, <code>suppression-without-reason</code>, and <code>unused-suppression</code> &mdash; so a suppression cannot rot silently after the underlying problem is fixed.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <section id="output" class="mb-16 pt-10 border-t border-stone-light">
                <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl font-bold text-base-content mb-6 flex items-center">
                    <span class="iconify text-primary mr-3 text-lg" data-icon="carbon:json" aria-hidden="true"></span> Output
                </h2>
                <p class="text-charcoal-mid mb-6 max-w-3xl">
                    Human findings render through the existing diagnostics reporter, so they inherit source snippets and the project's colour,
                    emoji, and accessibility policies, followed by a summary line stating the count at each severity and any truncation.
                    With <code>--json</code> the command emits exactly one document in the shared envelope. When no finding reaches the
                    threshold, a result document goes to stdout; when one does, a diagnostic document goes to stderr with the same finding
                    objects under <code>diagnostics[0].related</code>, preserving the envelope invariant every other command follows.
                </p>
                <div class="bg-base-content rounded-xl overflow-x-auto min-w-0 [contain:inline-size]" tabindex="0">
                    <div class="bg-charcoal-mid/20 px-4 py-2 border-b border-white/10 text-xs font-mono text-stone-light">netsuke --json check (passing, abridged from the design document; the URL resolves once #621 merges)</div>
                    {% highlight 'json' %}{% raw %}
{
  "schema_version": 1,
  "generator": { "name": "netsuke", "version": "0.4.0" },
  "result": {
    "command": "check",
    "status": "pass",
    "fail_on": "error",
    "summary": { "error": 0, "warning": 2, "advice": 1,
                 "reported": 3, "suppressed": 1, "omitted": 0 },
    "truncated": false,
    "findings": [
      {
        "message": "depends on the directory `build` through `deps`",
        "code": "netsuke::lint::directory_dep_not_order_only",
        "severity": "warning",
        "help": "Move the directory to `order_only_deps`, which guarantees it exists first without tracking its timestamp.",
        "url": "https://github.com/leynos/netsuke/blob/main/docs/netsuke-linter-rules.md#directory-dep-not-order-only",
        "primary_span": { "line": 24, "column": 7, "snippet": "      - \"{{ build_dir }}\"" }
      }
    ]
  }
}
{% endraw %}{% endhighlight %}
                </div>
            </section>

            <section id="shallow-end" class="mb-16 pt-10 border-t border-stone-light">
                <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl font-bold text-base-content mb-6 flex items-center">
                    <span class="iconify text-primary mr-3 text-lg" data-icon="carbon:flow" aria-hidden="true"></span> From the shallow end
                </h2>
                <p class="text-charcoal-mid mb-6 max-w-3xl">
                    The first step is one bare command with nothing to configure. <code>netsuke check</code> loads, expands, renders, and lowers
                    the manifest through the same stages as a build, with impure template helpers disabled; no recipe runs and no build output
                    is created. The branch&rsquo;s migration note says it plainly: existing manifests need no changes, because checking is
                    read-only. Everything past that first run is optional, and each step builds on the one before.
                </p>
                <ol class="space-y-4 mb-6 max-w-3xl">
                    <li class="bg-white p-5 rounded-xl border border-base-300 shadow-xs min-w-0">
                        <h3 class="font-bold text-base-content mb-1 text-sm">1. Run it and read what it says</h3>
                        <p class="text-xs text-charcoal-mid">No rule defaults to <code>error</code> and the default threshold is <code>error</code>, so on a manifest that compiles the first run reports findings without failing. Convention rules stay quiet: <code>unreachable-target</code> and <code>rule-without-description</code> are off until a selector enables them, because they encode a house style rather than a defect.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li class="bg-white p-5 rounded-xl border border-base-300 shadow-xs min-w-0">
                        <h3 class="font-bold text-base-content mb-1 text-sm">2. Ask why</h3>
                        <p class="text-xs text-charcoal-mid"><code>netsuke check --explain directory-dep-not-order-only</code> prints one rule&rsquo;s summary, rationale, and remediation; <code>--explain</code> alone lists the catalogue. The explanation ships in the binary, so it matches the rules that binary runs.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li class="bg-white p-5 rounded-xl border border-base-300 shadow-xs min-w-0">
                        <h3 class="font-bold text-base-content mb-1 text-sm">3. Make it a gate</h3>
                        <p class="text-xs text-charcoal-mid"><code>--fail-on warning</code> turns warnings into a failing exit code, which is the point at which the linter starts guarding a CI pipeline. <code>--fail-on never</code> goes the other way and makes findings purely informational.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li class="bg-white p-5 rounded-xl border border-base-300 shadow-xs min-w-0">
                        <h3 class="font-bold text-base-content mb-1 text-sm">4. Fix the policy once</h3>
                        <p class="text-xs text-charcoal-mid">Category and rule selectors narrow in order, and the <code>[cmds.check]</code> table in <code>netsuke.toml</code> records them for the project (see <a href="{{ fc.href('linter', 'policy') }}" class="hm-link">policy and suppression</a>). The table layers through the usual <a href="/netsuke/docs/configuration/" class="hm-link">configuration</a> precedence.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li class="bg-white p-5 rounded-xl border border-base-300 shadow-xs min-w-0">
                        <h3 class="font-bold text-base-content mb-1 text-sm">5. Suppress with a reason</h3>
                        <p class="text-xs text-charcoal-mid">Where a finding is deliberate, a <code># netsuke-lint: allow</code> directive names the rule and says why. The suppression rules keep those directives honest after the code around them changes.</p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
                <div class="bg-primary/5 border border-primary/20 rounded-lg p-5 flex items-start">
                    <span class="iconify text-primary mt-1 mr-3" data-icon="carbon:direction-fork" aria-hidden="true"></span>
                    <p class="text-xs text-charcoal-mid">
                        <strong>The deep end.</strong> <code>netsuke --json check --explain</code> emits the whole registry as data, which the
                        design calls the catalogue an editor or agent reads to build a rule picker without scraping prose. Together with the
                        per-finding span data, the design judges it sufficient for a language server; no such server is planned in this work.
                    </p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <section id="access" class="mb-16 pt-10 border-t border-stone-light">
                <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl font-bold text-base-content mb-6 flex items-center">
                    <span class="iconify text-primary mr-3 text-lg" data-icon="carbon:accessibility" aria-hidden="true"></span> Accessibility and localization
                </h2>
                <p class="text-charcoal-mid mb-6 max-w-3xl">
                    The linter adds no output mode of its own. Human findings go through the existing diagnostics reporter, so they inherit the
                    project&rsquo;s colour, emoji, and <a href="/netsuke/docs/cli/#accessible-output" class="hm-link">accessible output</a> policies.
                    The design document practises the same habit: its architecture diagram is preceded by a prose description for screen-reader users.
                </p>
                <div class="grid md:grid-cols-3 gap-6 mb-6">
                    <div class="bg-white p-6 rounded-xl border border-base-300 shadow-xs min-w-0">
                        <h3 class="font-bold text-base-content mb-2 text-sm">Framing text: localized now</h3>
                        <p class="text-xs text-charcoal-mid">The command&rsquo;s subcommand and flag help, threshold message, summary line, and policy errors are localized as usual. The pull request adds <code>check</code> keys to all 35 <a href="/netsuke/docs/cli/#localization" class="hm-link">Fluent catalogues</a>.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="bg-white p-6 rounded-xl border border-base-300 shadow-xs min-w-0">
                        <h3 class="font-bold text-base-content mb-2 text-sm">Rule prose: English for now</h3>
                        <p class="text-xs text-charcoal-mid">A rule&rsquo;s summary, rationale, and remediation are not localized in v0.4.0. ADR-021&rsquo;s reasoning: the prototype&rsquo;s wording is expected to change, and translating prose before it settles spends the effort twice.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="bg-white p-6 rounded-xl border border-base-300 shadow-xs min-w-0">
                        <h3 class="font-bold text-base-content mb-2 text-sm">Identifiers: never translated</h3>
                        <p class="text-xs text-charcoal-mid">Rule, category, and severity names and diagnostic codes are values a user types into a configuration file or a suppression comment and a machine matches exactly. They stay untranslated permanently.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <p class="text-sm text-charcoal-mid max-w-3xl">
                    Step 12.2 of the branch&rsquo;s roadmap plans the migration: Fluent keys of the form <code>lint.rule.&lt;name&gt;.summary</code>,
                    <code>.rationale</code>, and <code>.remediation</code>, with the English text kept as the fallback so a catalogue gap degrades to
                    the source locale rather than to an empty diagnostic. Under <code>--locale</code>, a finding&rsquo;s <code>rule</code>,
                    <code>category</code>, <code>severity</code>, and <code>code</code> are to stay byte-identical across locales, and
                    <code>--explain --json</code> stays in the source locale so an editor&rsquo;s rule picker gets stable text. Translation follows the
                    project&rsquo;s localization glossary and style guide, leaving manifest identifiers such as <code>order_only_deps</code> verbatim.
                </p>
            </section>

            <section id="architecture" class="mb-16 pt-10 border-t border-stone-light">
                <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl font-bold text-base-content mb-6 flex items-center">
                    <span class="iconify text-primary mr-3 text-lg" data-icon="carbon:flow" aria-hidden="true"></span> How it works
                </h2>
                <p class="text-charcoal-mid mb-6 max-w-3xl">
                    The linter binds to four points in the pipeline described in <a href="/netsuke/guides/architecture/" class="hm-link">How Netsuke Works</a>.
                    Manifest source flows into a span index and, in parallel, through the existing compiler stages; each lint stage consumes the
                    artefact produced immediately above it, and all four feed one ordered finding sink.
                </p>
                <div class="grid md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 mb-8">
                    <div class="bg-base-100 p-5 rounded-lg border border-base-300">
                        <div class="font-mono text-xs text-primary mb-2">document</div>
                        <p class="text-xs text-charcoal-mid">Rules over the authored source with spans: shell constructs, placeholders, unused declarations, redundant flags.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="bg-base-100 p-5 rounded-lg border border-base-300">
                        <div class="font-mono text-xs text-primary mb-2">manifest</div>
                        <p class="text-xs text-charcoal-mid">Rules over the expanded, typed manifest: dependency shapes, duplicate recipes, unused rules.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="bg-base-100 p-5 rounded-lg border border-base-300">
                        <div class="font-mono text-xs text-primary mb-2">graph</div>
                        <p class="text-xs text-charcoal-mid">Rules over the lowered build graph: undeclared inputs and unreachable targets.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="bg-base-100 p-5 rounded-lg border border-base-300">
                        <div class="font-mono text-xs text-primary mb-2">directive</div>
                        <p class="text-xs text-charcoal-mid">Rules over the suppression directives themselves.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <p class="text-sm text-charcoal-mid max-w-3xl">
                    A rule states what it found and where; the engine stamps the severity resolved from policy, so no rule can decide how loudly to
                    speak. Every rule carries a summary, a rationale, and a remediation in its registry entry, and a contract test checks the rule
                    reference against that registry in both directions, so it can neither omit a shipped rule nor document one that does not exist.
                    A future span-preserving YAML rewriter could add autofixes; that is a separate project and not part of this preview.
                </p>
                <h3 class="font-bold text-base-content mt-8 mb-4 text-sm uppercase tracking-wide">How the design earns trust</h3>
                <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-6">
                    <div class="bg-white p-6 rounded-xl border border-base-300 shadow-xs min-w-0">
                        <h4 class="font-bold text-base-content mb-2 text-sm">Three tests per rule, at minimum</h4>
                        <p class="text-xs text-charcoal-mid">A positive case that must fire, a negative case that must not, and a suppression case proving the directive silences it &mdash; and that removing the reason produces <code>suppression-without-reason</code>. Snapshot tests pin the human rendering and both JSON branches; behavioural tests drive the binary end to end; and the repository&rsquo;s own example manifests are linted by a test.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="bg-white p-6 rounded-xl border border-base-300 shadow-xs min-w-0">
                        <h4 class="font-bold text-base-content mb-2 text-sm">Properties over generated manifests</h4>
                        <p class="text-xs text-charcoal-mid">Findings are ordered deterministically; a suppression never reaches outside its node; <code>--limit</code> never changes which findings would have been reported; and a finding&rsquo;s severity equals the policy for its rule. Two runs over the same manifest and configuration produce byte-identical findings.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="bg-white p-6 rounded-xl border border-base-300 shadow-xs min-w-0">
                        <h4 class="font-bold text-base-content mb-2 text-sm">Honest spans, loud typos</h4>
                        <p class="text-xs text-charcoal-mid">A wrong span is worse than no span, so the resolver returns nothing rather than guessing. An unknown rule name in a selector is an error, so a typo in CI configuration fails loudly instead of silently disabling nothing.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="bg-white p-6 rounded-xl border border-base-300 shadow-xs min-w-0">
                        <h4 class="font-bold text-base-content mb-2 text-sm">Evidence before the freeze</h4>
                        <p class="text-xs text-charcoal-mid">The branch roadmap measures value by findings that changed a manifest, not by the number of rules registered. Its first step lints a corpus and records each rule&rsquo;s disposition before any contract is frozen. Two structural tasks are already done there: moving <code>miette</code> out of the lint core into an adapter, and runner telemetry for <code>netsuke check</code>.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </section>

{{ fc.footer('linter') }}

        </main>
    </div>

{% endblock %}
